a few months ago i made a video about a  difficult turkish exam question and that  video seemed to resonate with a lot of  people there were comments in turkish  that were glad that people could see the  pain that they had to go through and  there were plenty of comments lamenting  the state of the education system let me  show you some of the comments that  gained a lot of support this one  translates to you only have a black pen  but create a rainbow this is a summary  of the turkish education system and this  one says that the questions are of high  quality but unfortunately there is no  system that will take us to the answers  of those questions now i have never  studied in turkey and i don't know  enough to agree or disagree with these  statements but they do relate to a  larger sentiment that we see in many  other countries  and that is that the exam is not the  challenge the real challenge is  competing against the other students the  exam in question is the ayt paper used  to gain entrance into turkish  universities  the population of turkey is 85 million  and over two million students are taking  this test hoping to be in the top 50 000  to get into the good universities when  entry is so competitive you see exams  getting harder to try and separate  people but many are left feeling  frustrated by the fact that making exams  harder doesn't raise the quality of  education and instead can leave students  feeling dejected and hating school this  comment said geography is destiny and  this one said may god help turkish  indian and south korean students  assumably talking about the tough exams  they all have another common thread was  people saying that even if you're lucky  enough to ace this exam get into a good  university and graduate that there are  still not many jobs waiting for you at  the end this oecd unemployment rate data  does show that turkey has a pretty high  unemployment rate the latest figures are  around 11.2 percent whilst the usa and  the uk are at four percent and australia  4.2  it also seems like a lot of the top  students aspire to go overseas  contributing to what they call brain  drain where a country's brightest people  aren't sticking around to help it grow i  plan to work through another one of the  tough questions from the ayt exam paper  and i will get to doing that in this  video but first i got distracted by  another comment that told me to check  out the lgs exam given to middle  schoolers in turkey as a high school  entrance exam it is very hard a math  question this year was taken from the  university exam they are literally  forcing students to solve university  level questions at age 13 to 14. so  naturally i wanted to take a look here's  the paper and yes it's really hard  apparently students studying for this  are putting in eight plus hour study  days making it the youngest example of  that kind of hard work i've seen for an  exam yet let's take a look at the last  question on the math section we've got  these pie charts which show the  distribution of televisions sold in an  electronic store in 2019 and 2020 k l  and m are three different types of  televisions it says that while the  number of l brand televisions sold in  the store in 2020 decreased by 25  compared to 2019  the m brand televisions increased in  number by 40. according to this  what is the number of k brand  televisions sold in 2019 so we want to  know how many televisions does this  slice of pie represent that would be  easy to do if for example we knew how  many televisions were sold in total  either in 2019 or 2020 but that's not  information that we have so this becomes  quite tricky please do have a go at  solving it and while you're at it i  would like to quickly thank the sponsor  of this video babel i've done a lot of  translating to try and read these exam  questions and the comments and i've  actually been using babble to learn just  the basics from the turkish language to  make it easier for me to pick out  keywords babel is a top language  learning app that teaches real-world  practical conversations through  10-minute interactive lessons as well as  podcasts games videos and live classes  babel teaches more than just vocab words  and also teaches you about the languages  culture people history and traditions  [Music]  learning a new language is the perfect  way to work on yourself in 2022 and  rabble is offering a 20-day money-back  guarantee  you can get 65 percent off by going to  my link which is down in the description  and now let's take a look at the  solution so there might be a number of  ways to solve it but the simplest way i  found was to try and find out how many  tvs each degree of the pie represents  i'm going to define this variable x  which is how many tvs there are in each  degree of the pie in 2019 and also i'm  going to define y which is the same  thing but for the 2020 pie chart now  let's look at the chart we will want to  know how many degrees are in this  section k  at first glance it might look like it's  a third 120 like this one down here but  actually it's not if this one here l is  90 degrees  m is 120 and in total this has to add to  360 then k will actually be  150 degrees over here it's a bit more  simple we've got 90 90 and so this one  would have to be 180. if we can find x  then the problem will be solved by  finding 150 times x that will be how  many tvs this chunk represents if that's  150x then this one here is 90x 120x  since the number of m brand televisions  increases in number by 40  then this section here will be  120x  plus 40. and since l decreases in number  by 25 this one here will be  90x minus 25. but these segments can  also be represented using y  this one is also  90 degrees times y and this one down  here is 180y  and since these represent the same  number of tvs in this chunk we can write  90x minus 25 is equal to 90y  and for this one 120x  plus 40  is equal to 180y this is our key to  solving the problem we've got two  unknowns but also two equations so we  can solve this for both x and y  rearranging the top one for y and  subbing that into the second one i get x  to be three over two remember what we  wanted to solve the problem was 150x so  150  times 3 over 2 well that is 450 over 2  which is equal to 225  meaning that c is the correct answer now  it took me a few goes to get there but  if you found this easily then  congratulations you're as smart as a  turkish 13 year old i can't help but  think that if you already are studying  so hard for exams this early that by the  time you get to those tougher university  entrance exams you may have already  burnt out but it seems like even getting  into some of the best high schools is a  competitive process this is a question  from that notorious university entrance  exam this is from the 2021 ayt  mathematics paper  translated it says that p of x is a  fourth degree polynomial with real  coefficients and it gives the inequality  that p of x is bigger than or equal to x  it gives us three values of p of x at x  is one two and three and it asks us to  find the value of p of x at x is equal  to 4. now on an axis of x and p of x  this line here represents where p of x  is equal to x a function is called p of  x but it could just as easily have been  called y and you may recognize this as  more commonly called the line y equals x  this inequality tells us that the value  of our function will always exist above  or at this line so in this region here  in fact we can plot some of the points  at x is equal to one p of x is also  equal to one so that point is on the  line the same thing happens at x is  equal to three in between at x is equal  to two the value of p of x is 4 so it  will be somewhere up here and since we  know it's a 4th degree polynomial or a  quartic we know it'll be a wibbly-wobbly  shape but because it can't pass down  into this region down here it must look  something like this  and our question wants us to find the  value when x is equal to 4 so when it's  somewhere up here finding the value of  this polynomial at any x value would be  easy if only we had the equation of this  line but the equation of this line is  slightly hard to find when we look at it  from this perspective instead let's take  a look at our inequality if we subtract  x from both sides we have that p of x  minus x is bigger than or equal to zero  subtracting x off this function makes it  something that's easier to find an  equation for because it will intersect  the x-axis plotting p of x minus x we  get something that looks more like this  and you can see where i've got these  points from i just subtracted x from  these values here 1 minus 1 would give  us a value of 0 there 4 minus 2 gives  the coordinate 2 2  and 3 minus 3 gives the coordinate 3 0  i know the x intercepts of this quartic  so i can actually write its equation  i'd get k which is some scale factor  that we're yet to find  x minus 1 squared times x minus 3  squared if you're slightly unfamiliar  with cortex you may wonder how i know  that these are both squared well i can  tell what they call the multiplicity of  these zeros by actually looking at the  graph  also called the order the multiplicity  is how many zeros are at the x-intercept  for example if the multiplicity was 1  that would be if this bracket wasn't  squared it was just a single value of x  in that case the x-intercept would be  cut in a linear fashion the intercept  would look something like this or like  this  with a multiplicity of 2 that's when we  would see an x squared and the intercept  would look like a quadratic something  like this or this this is for a  multiplicity of 2 but also 4  6 8 and in fact any even number and a  multiplicity of 3  or of 5  7 9 and any other odd number we would  see something that looks like this where  the intercept is in there fun fact is  the higher this number of multiplicity  the flatter the section will look our  x-intercepts come down and turn around  and that sort of quadratic function so  that's why we can say that that would be  squared if we're dealing with a cortex  that is a power of x to the 4 then the  total multiplicities added together will  equal 4. we do still want to find the  value of k and we can do that using  these points here if we used x equals 1  we'd end up with 1 minus 1 is 0 that  would all be 0 and it wouldn't be useful  for finding k same thing would happen if  we used 3 that's probably why they've  given us this one in between if p of two  minus two is equal to two  we'd have k  two minus one is one squared  two minus 3  is minus 1 squared that would all be 1  and k would just be equal to 2. so this  would be our final equation for p of x  minus x  and that's all we need to be able to  find p of 4. plugging in x is equal to 4  it works out to give us the answer of  22. luckily that's one of our options  and so we can say that b is the right  answer i know that in the real exam you  get a lot less time than i've taken here  but i'm trying to explain things as i go  especially if this problem is an  unfamiliar type for you what makes the  exam even harder is that this might be  an unfamiliar question to almost  everyone it doesn't look like it appears  in last year's exam and so there's not  really any warning to prepare for  something like this  interestingly this exam made the news in  turkey this week after a decision from  the council of higher education to  change the minimum passing score  whilst entry to the top universities  will remain very competitive students  could still be placed in a less  competitive program after getting as  little as one question correct  in turkey there are many higher  education institutes but they're not all  of the same quality  perhaps this decision could lead to more  low quality institutes offering degrees  that don't help to reduce the country's  unemployment  or we'll see private universities now  accepting anyone who's willing to pay  the tuition fee  thanks for watching this video and again  let me know your thoughts down in the  comments this video is in honor of  today's patreon cat of the day latte  sent in by connor  [Music]  you

